WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.474
Mainframes.

00:00:01.474 --> 00:00:02.702
What about Microsoft Cloud?

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.171
Mainframes are critical to your business.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.708
They're secure, reliable and scalable
for all your most important

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:12.245
applications
and data. To continue to build on this investment.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:18.251
businesses are looking to extend core applications
in a hybrid cloud model as part of Microsoft Cloud.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.575
I agree, that's a smart strategy.

00:00:20.773 --> 00:00:24.357
Right! By introducing your mainframe developers
to Microsoft Cloud,

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:28.661
you're leveraging modern tools
and removing silos in your development process.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:31.231
Hi, I'm Brian Lang from IBM.

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.967
And I'm Shelby Howard from Microsoft.

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.603
Today, we're going to explain
how we're partnering to bring the strengths

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:40.959
of both IBM Z and Microsoft Cloud to our clients. Ready? 

00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:42.242
You bet.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.911
So we're already working together by delivering IBM Z

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.881
and the Cloud Modernization
Stack through Azure Marketplace.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:51.551
Let's walk through a realistic problem
that our partnership can solve.

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:56.122
So imagine a scenario
where a client is a manufacturing client.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:01:00.927
They have dealerships referencing their parts systems,
but their supply chain issues.

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:06.666
So those dealerships are actually hitting the IBM
Z looking for parts as they come in

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.070
because there's a shortage and they're really hammering
the mainframe to get that inventory.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.739
Well, the IBM Z is really overworked in terms of consumption.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.341
It's really delivering a bunch of non-update transactions.

00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:19.145
Yeah. And so there's potentially a need for additional capacity.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:25.285
But to solve a short term issue, because it's ideally not
a long term issue with supply chain.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:29.222
So how do we help this customer solve the problem?

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:33.359
So we're able to add agility by creating an API in Azure

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.529
and directing that traffic to Azure.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:40.233
And this update happens as the inventory is replenished.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:45.004
So we're timing the update through the API
and leveraging Azure API management.

00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:50.076
Okay, so now we have an API that's running in the Microsoft Cloud

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.880
and it's basically servicing all of the query traffic

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:59.252
that's coming from those dealers without impacting the IBM
Z, The IBM Z really gets to sit back and relax.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:00.487
Yeah, exactly.

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:04.224
So we're getting the value
that the dealerships need, being able to

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.026
purchase that inventory as it comes up.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:10.029
But we're not driving that consumption
on the mainframe.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.532
And the IBM Z is not really sitting back and relaxing,

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.200
it's driving a bunch of transactions.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.538
And obviously one of those transactions is going to be
when that inventory gets updated, it

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:21.908
now updates the API, feeding the dealership.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:24.297
But it's a purposeful transaction. 

00:02:24.297 --> 00:02:28.381
Absolutely. So now that we've got our client developed

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.719
developing code in that Microsoft Cloud with the Mod Stack,

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:36.289
they've got an API,
maybe they've expanded that to do multiple APIs.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:40.126
Take us through how they start that process.

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.794
How do they get there?

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:47.667
So the design and develop is actually happening in Azure
and leveraging the Mod Stack.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:53.940
Okay. So I've got a developer interacting with the Mod Stack,
running in the Microsoft Cloud.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:55.942
I've designed I've developed it.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:56.743
What's the next step?

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.878
The next step is going to be to deploy the API.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:03.016
Okay, so now I've got that API running in the Microsoft
Cloud, again,

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:09.055
servicing in between the IBM Z and whatever
the end user may be, in this case, our dealership.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.090
But really, how does

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.226
anybody even know that that API has been developed?

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:17.564
So the next step is to expose the API,

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:21.100
but you've deployed the API / runtime in Azure.

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:27.473
And also one thing to mention here
is that the API is leveraging Open API 3 standards.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:28.274
Okay.

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.111
So really the only reason why
we've been able to integrate these things

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:37.150
between Microsoft Cloud and IBM Z is
because we're all supporting open industry standards.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.119
So now we're going to expose that API, correct?

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:43.022
Yes. And you're exposing it, leveraging Azure API management,

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:47.961
and that gives you consistency across your environment,
managing all of your APIs.

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:48.828
Okay.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.129
So who are we exposing it to?

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:54.567
Obviously, we're exposing it to the dealerships
in this example so that they're updating their code,

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:57.904
make sure that they're accessing the API as opposed to IBM
Z directly.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:00.089
Who else might want access to that API?

00:04:00.089 --> 00:04:05.511
That's a good point. So we've solved the problem here
by exposing the API for this scenario.

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:10.016
But that API can also be leveraged for new capability.

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:11.918
And let's say the client wants to

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:16.256
create a Power App using low-code/no code.

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:18.591
They could also leverage that API in the future. 

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.793
Okay. So now we're extending that ability.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:25.164
We're taking the taking the investment
that a client's had in Mod Stack and in Microsoft

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:30.703
Cloud--extending the value of IBM Z, not only for this
for this problem and solving this problem,

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:34.907
but now solving multiple problems and expanding that value

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.143
into other other realms, really.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.280
What other use cases do we have that

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.982
we can talk about with our clients?

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.451
Yeah, so we're talking about APIs.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:49.522
One of the other things to mention about Azure
API management is that you also have the ability

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:53.026
to deploy security policies using that and control traffic.

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:57.864
So having that consistency is key for development
across the enterprise.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.600
The next thing is actually DevOps.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.371
So expanding on development,
we can bring the mainframe development

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.907
into Azure, leveraging the Mod Stack

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:11.311
and create new capability there too.

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:14.881
So Mod Stack supports both modern languages.

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:18.718
We're talking Java Go, Node.js, but also COBOL, right?

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:24.857
So you can update your zOS applications using Mod
Stack with your developers running in Microsoft Cloud.

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:28.928
Yes. And going back to the example where we have an API

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:32.632
that's exposed to maybe a new application in Azure,

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.802
you also are removing barriers across

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:38.838
scrum teams by having cloud native development

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:42.842
being done in other areas
as well as through the mainframe team.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:47.046
And again, because it's open industry
standards tied in with Azure DevOps,

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:51.651
open industry standards, that code can be written there,
the code can be developed there,

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:56.356
and then the application can be deployed back to IBM
Z, running on the mainframe.

00:05:56.656 --> 00:06:00.293
Yeah. And one of the other important things
is that you have testing that happens:

00:06:00.293 --> 00:06:04.564
end-to-end testing in Azure leveraging a Z sandbox.

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:07.767
So unit testing on there
you're obviously going to bring that back

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:11.070
and do your integration and production
level testing back on IBM Z.

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:13.740
Fantastic. Any other use cases that we have?

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:17.610
Yes. One other use case that we have is optimizing storage.

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:22.615
So when we look at this,
we're talking about optimizing tape storage or disk storage

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:27.453
by leveraging Azure blob storage
and directing that storage into Azure.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.924
So in that case,
you're really taking an alternative approach to putting

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.527
some cold storage into the Microsoft Cloud.

00:06:34.794 --> 00:06:39.732
Obviously, if you need to do any kind of analytics
or any kind of data update to that, you actually have to

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:45.471
bring that that data back to the back to the mainframe
to actually do transaction processing against it.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:50.543
Yes, these cases disk optimization,
but you also have the ability to have an additional

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:55.014
or redundant storage
copy if maybe it's a regulatory requirement.

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:55.982
Great.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:58.050
So these are the three patterns
that we've already delivered.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.319
They're already available in the Microsoft Cloud.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:04.490
Looking forward to expanding that partnership
as we solve more problems for our customers.

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.192
I'll give you the last word.

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:06.793
Thank you.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:10.396
So we have a lot of our clients

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:14.100
investing in both the IBM Z and Microsoft Cloud.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:18.638
And we walk through an example scenario today
where we were solving a problem for a client.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:21.808
We can also help you solve a real world

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:25.711
example for your environment.
And not only just solving a problem--

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:29.449
one of the things that I would encourage
is that you look for additional capability

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.083
outside of just solving a problem.

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.720
Now, knowing that the two platforms
can interact a lot more closely.

00:07:34.921 --> 00:07:39.025
There may be a different way to look at your environment
and adding additional value to your business.

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:40.605
Thanks for watching.

00:07:40.605 --> 00:07:44.697
Before you leave, don't forget to hit like and subscribe.

